  <Icon>k__BackingField: {fileID: -3438905571476058607, guid: f95da810d1268624c80d5c367d2e9f52, type: 3}
  <AboutText>k__BackingField: '<b><size=+10>Overview</size></b>

    Spider Solitaire
    is beloved by card players of all skill levels, containing numerous variants
    to ensure that everyone can enjoy it. It requires two standard 52-card decks
    (totaling 104 cards) with 54 cards dealt across 10 columns.

    

    Spider
    requires players to sort cards into same-suit stacks from King to Ace. Cards
    are arranged on the board in descending order, so players must manoeuvre the
    cards until they have a complete same-suit stack, causing those cards to be removed
    from the game. Victory is achieved when all 104 cards have been sorted. Spider
    is primarily a skill-based game, but luck still plays a part in whether or not
    you''ll win.


    <b><size=+10>History</size></b>

    Spider Solitaire is quite
    young, with our earliest record of the game found in Ely Culbertson''s "Card
    Games Complete with Official Rules" which was published in 1917. However, this
    early version contained several differences to our modern rules and was significantly
    harder. Despite the difficulty, Spider is said to have been a favourite pastime
    of US President Franklin D. Roosevelt, who would use the game to unwind during
    the 1930s and 1940s.

    

    Over the years, the game continued to evolve,
    until finally, in 1949, the rules that we still use today were published in "The
    Complete Book of Solitaire and Patience Games" written by Albert H. Morehead
    and Geoffrey Mott-Smith. This version expanded on the game, modifying some existing
    rules, and provided several variations to ensure that people of various skill
    levels could enjoy the game.

    

    Unlike with some other Solitaire games,
    there is no particular story or event tied to this game''s name. It is quite
    simple: just as a spider has eight legs, Spider Solitaire requires eight stacks
    to be made.


    <b><size=+10>Today</size></b>

    Spider is one of the most
    accessible versions of Solitaire, with variants such as one-suit ensuring that
    anybody can play. Like several other Solitaire games, Spider can attribute its
    popularity to its inclusion as a free game in early versions of Microsoft Windows.
    Despite its relative youth, Spider has long cemented its place as one of the
    most popular single-player card games in the world.'
  <GuideText>k__BackingField: 
